    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\nPlain prose only: no headers, no bullet points, no bold text, no em dashes. Write like a person.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nMost product roadmaps fail for a simple reason: they are asked to do two jobs that pull in opposite directions. One job is to help a team decide what to build next. The other is to reassure people who are not on the team that the future is under control. The second job almost always wins, and once it does, the roadmap stops being a tool and becomes a performance.\n\nYou can see this in how roadmaps get made. Someone senior wants to know what is shipping in Q3. A product manager, wanting to look competent, fills in the boxes. Sales wants a date for the feature a big customer asked about, so a date appears. Engineering is asked to estimate work that has not been scoped, and they guess, knowing the guess will be treated as a commitment. By the time the document is finished, it is a list of promises made to people who each heard what they wanted to hear. Nobody lied, exactly. But nobody was really planning either.\n\nThe deeper problem is that a roadmap encodes a level of certainty that does not exist. Six months out, you do not know what customers will ask for, what a competitor will launch, which assumption in your current bet will turn out to be wrong, or which two engineers will leave. Everyone knows this. Yet the format of a roadmap, with its neat quarters and confident labels, quietly erases that knowledge. It looks like a schedule, so it gets treated like one. When reality diverges, which it always does, the roadmap is either abandoned or defended. Both outcomes are bad. Abandoned, it teaches the organization that planning is theater. Defended, it forces teams to ship things they already know are wrong because changing course would look like failure.\n\nRoadmaps also tend to list features instead of problems. A feature is easy to put on a timeline and easy to check off. A problem is messier, because you might discover halfway through that the feature you picked does not actually solve it. But a feature list gives a team no way to think. When the plan says \"build export to CSV\" and a user interview reveals that nobody actually wants CSV, they want to stop needing the export at all, the roadmap has no room for that finding. It was written to be executed, not to be learned from.\n\nAnd then there is capacity. Almost every roadmap I have seen assumes the team spends all of its time on roadmap items. It ignores bugs, support escalations, the migration that cannot wait, the thing the CEO saw at a conference. Unplanned work is not an exception; it is a fixed cost, usually a third of the team's time or more. A plan that pretends otherwise is late from the day it is published.\n\nNone of this means roadmaps are useless. A good one states what the team is trying to learn and what it currently believes, and it invites disagreement. It gets shorter and vaguer the further out it goes, because that is honest. The roadmaps that fail are the ones that were never allowed to be honest in the first place.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they treat the future as something we can schedule. Teams spend weeks lining up features and dates as if they already know what customers will need six months from now and how long every piece of work will take. Then the world moves. A new competitor appears, a key assumption turns out to be wrong, or someone important leaves the company. The carefully drawn plan starts looking like fiction.\n\nPeople also turn the roadmap into a commitment instead of a working hypothesis. Sales uses the dates to close deals. Leadership uses the document to judge whether the team is delivering. When the inevitable changes happen, trust disappears. The thing that was supposed to keep everyone aligned becomes the thing everyone argues about.\n\nA lot of roadmaps describe solutions rather than the problems worth solving. They become a list of screens and capabilities decided last quarter. Fresh evidence shows up, but the team keeps executing the old list because changing the roadmap feels like admitting failure. The document stops helping people think and starts telling them what to build no matter what they learn.\n\nWe consistently underestimate complexity. Work takes longer than the slides suggested. Dependencies surface late. Maintenance and technical debt that were never on the roadmap suddenly demand attention. Discovery that would reduce risk gets skipped because it does not look like progress against the published plan.\n\nCommunication gaps finish the job. The group that created the roadmap rarely stays in close conversation with the people building the product or talking to customers every day. Hidden assumptions stay hidden until they blow up. Trade-offs that should have been explicit stay implicit until it is too late.\n\nThe roadmaps that survive are the ones treated as living sketches rather than finished paintings. They stay detailed only for the near term and stay deliberately vague further out. They get revised often. They focus more on the outcomes the business needs than on the exact features that might produce those outcomes. Most companies do not operate that way. They produce a clean looking artifact, present it, and then wonder why it did not last. Planning gives the comforting feeling of control. That feeling is usually an illusion, and that is why most product roadmaps fail.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
